{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_16","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"rg --files bin lib | rg -v 'cljd-out|\\\\.g\\\\.' | head -65; rg -n 'register.bench|yin/vm/(semantic|register|stack|space|macro|wasm)|continuation.handoff|yin.vm.bytecode-bench|#(pipeline|continuation|plotter)([\"'^-]|$)'\"' bin lib public --glob '\"'!cljd-out/**'\"' --glob '\"'!*.js'\"' --glob '\"'!*.map'\"' --glob '\"'!*.svg'\"' --glob '\"'!*.json'\"' --glob '\"'!*.lock'\"' --glob '\"'!*.html'\"' --max-count 3 | head -45; nl -ba docs/agents/architecture.md | sed -n '1,95p'; rg -n 'register-bench|yin.vm.(semantic|register|stack|space|macro|wasm)' .gitignore src/dev/clean.clj; nl -ba bin/register_bench_cljd.dart\"","aggregated_output":"lib/main.dart\nbin/yin_repl_main.dart\nbin/run-ios-simulator.sh\nbin/cljs-demo-build.sh\nbin/yin_repl_main.dart\nbin/register_bench_cljd.dart\nbin/clean.sh\nbin/bench.dart\nbin/run-ws-demo.sh\nbin/run-android-emulator.sh\nbin/start-web-server.sh\nbin/watch.sh\nbin/start-ws-server.sh\nbin/analyze-deps.clj\nbin/redeploy.sh\nbin/register_bench_cljd_v2.dart\nbin/compile-shaders.sh\nbin/run-cljs-tests.sh\nbin/run-ws-client.sh\nbin/download-flutter-mirror.sh\nbin/register_bench_cljd.dart:1:import '../lib/cljd-out/yin/register-bench-cljd.dart' as bench;\npublic/chp/yin.chp:18:         [:p [:a.cta-button {:href \"/demo.html#pipeline\"} \"Try the Live Demo\"]]]]\nbin/register_bench_cljd_v2.dart:1:import '../lib/cljd-out/yin/register-bench-cljd-v2.dart' as bench;\n     1\t---\n     2\tdescription: System architecture - streams, agents/continuations, YIN.VM CESK machine, compilation pipeline\n     3\t---\n     4\t\n     5\t# STREAMS\n     6\t\n     7\tAll IO is modeled as a stream.\n     8\tFunctions consume streams and produce streams.\n     9\tNo direct function-to-function coupling without a stream boundary.\n    10\tSide effects must appear as stream emissions.\n    11\tOrdering guarantees must be explicit.\n    12\tStreams are values that can be sent through streams.\n    13\t\n    14\tBounded vs Unbounded:\n    15\t  Open stream: unbounded, still receiving datoms.\n    16\t  Bounded stream: closed at an exact finite bound, stable for reasoning.\n    17\t  A bounded stream IS the database value (no separate \"value\" type).\n    18\t  Stability comes from bounding, not from a different abstraction.\n    19\t\n    20\tStream Iteration:\n    21\t  A stream's order is declared by its descriptor or interpreter.\n    22\t  There is no universal tuple order and no position has intrinsic meaning.\n    23\t  An append log may expose append order; a covered index may expose EAVT order.\n    24\t  Consumers that depend on order require explicit comparator metadata.\n    25\t\n    26\tStreams and Indexes:\n    27\t  Streams are the universal abstraction (datoms in t-order).\n    28\t  Indexes are optional optimizations built by publishers (e.g., dao.space.index).\n    29\t  dao.space.query/q works on any bounded DaoStream descriptor:\n    30\t    - Unindexed stream: O(n) scan\n    31\t    - Published index stream: EAVT/AEVT/AVET/VAET indexes, O(log n) lookup\n    32\t  Same interface, same semantics, different performance.\n    33\t  Indexes are a performance optimization, not a semantic requirement.\n    34\t\n    35\t# AGENTS\n    36\t\n    37\tAgents, functions, closures, and continuations are a unified concept in Yin.VM.\n    38\tAgents may migrate across nodes.\n    39\tAgents own state through their continuation structure: call stack, environment, and stored values.\n    40\tAgents communicate with the outside world through DaoStream.\n    41\tAgent behavior is specified entirely through stream effects.\n    42\tAgent identity must not imply trust.\n    43\tCapability tokens define authority, not identity.\n    44\t\n    45\tContinuation Migration:\n    46\t  When a continuation migrates, the AST datoms are the canonical payload.\n    47\t  Bytecode is a projection of the datoms for a specific execution model.\n    48\t  Migrating bytecode alone would force the destination to use the same VM.\n    49\t  Migrating datoms lets the destination project into whatever model it prefers.\n    50\t  The AST datoms can travel alone, giving the destination freedom to compile as needed.\n    51\t  Bytecode may accompany the datoms as a cache, but never travels without them.\n    52\t  If the destination runs the same VM model, it can use the bytecode directly.\n    53\t  If it doesn't, it recompiles from the datoms.\n    54\t  Closure fields like :body-bytes and :body-addr are ephemeral, local to the VM instance.\n    55\t  What survives migration: the datom stream, the captured environment bindings,\n    56\t  and the continuation structure (where execution was suspended).\n    57\t\n    58\tRuntime State:\n    59\t  Runtime state (continuations, environments, stack frames) is ephemeral.\n    60\t  Runtime state lives in memory as native VM data structures, not in dao.jing.\n    61\t  dao.jing is for persistent facts: AST datoms, schema, parked continuations.\n    62\t  Runtime state is queryable via Datalog on demand:\n    63\t    vm/state->datom-stream projects in-memory VM state into a datom stream.\n    64\t    Zero cost during execution; projection computed only when queried.\n    65\t    Joins across $code (dao.jing) and $runtime (in-memory stream) in a single dao.space.query/q:\n    66\t      [:find ?node-type ?frame-type\n    67\t       :in $code $runtime\n    68\t       :where [$runtime ?frame :cont/pending-arg [0 ?node]]\n    69\t              [$code ?node :yin/type ?node-type]\n    70\t              [$runtime ?frame :cont/type ?frame-type]]\n    71\t  Persistence boundary: park/migrate promotes ephemeral state to persistent datoms in dao.jing.\n    72\t\n    73\t# YIN.VM & UNIVERSAL AST\n    74\t\n    75\tYin.vm is a CESK continuation machine (Control, Environment, Store, Continuation).\n    76\tThe Universal AST is canonical code: syntax is a rendering.\n    77\tAST nodes are represented in two equivalent forms:\n    78\t  - AST maps: raw in-memory maps with :type, :operator, :operands, :body, etc.\n    79\t  - Datoms: [e a v t m] tuples stored in dao.jing and queryable via dao.space.query (Datalog).\n    80\tThe AST is not compiled away: it persists alongside execution state.\n    81\t\n    82\tASTs are materialized views over five orthogonal dimensions:\n    83\t  1. Structure (spatial): parent-child relationships, tree topology\n    84\t  2. Time (temporal): evolution through transactions\n    85\t  3. Type (certainty): static to dynamic as a continuum, not a binary\n    86\t  4. Language (transformations): cross-language semantic preservation\n    87\t  5. Execution (runtime): continuation state, stack frames, instruction pointers\n    88\t\n    89\tTwo independent interpreters prove the same semantics on different representations:\n    90\t  - ASTWalkerVM interprets AST maps via tree traversal (in-memory graphs).\n    91\t  - SemanticVM interprets AST datoms via graph traversal (dao.space.query).\n    92\tBoth are executable; neither is compiled away. They are equivalent views of the same computation.\n    93\t\n    94\tThe AST also enables multiple compilation backends via semantic projections:\n    95\t  - A stack-based VM that compiles AST datoms to stack bytecode.\n     1\timport '../lib/cljd-out/yin/register-bench-cljd.dart' as bench;\n     2\t\n     3\tvoid main() {\n     4\t  bench.main();\n     5\t}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
